      text: "It is impossible for one neighbor to be succored by another in his need, unless someone perform the work of mercy for him. Wherefore, since the precepts of the Law are about acts of virtue, it follows of necessity that all the acts of mercy fall under the precept, in so far, to wit, as they are necessary to virtue, namely, as they are demanded by right reason. Now according to right reason one should take account both of the requirements of the giver and of the needs of the recipient. Wherefore almsgiving is a matter of precept when the giver has goods which are not necessary for the upkeep of his own life, and when the recipient is in such need that it is not easy to see how his need may be otherwise relieved. Hence Ambrose says: \"Feed him that dies of hunger; if thou hast not fed him, thou hast slain him.\"",
