    meditation: `Isaias does not begin his prophecy with theology. He begins with a list. Wash. Be clean. Cease. Learn. Seek judgment. Relieve the oppressed. Judge for the fatherless. Defend the widow. The imperatives are short and the objects are specific — not "justice" in the abstract but the fatherless, in the singular, whose name was on the docket the day the prophet wrote. This is how the prophets work. They prefer particular nouns.

Open a week on the poor with that list, because the list is the lawyer's job description, and most of us, most days, have wandered some distance from it.

Start by being honest about whom the law currently serves. The American legal profession, taken as a whole, serves principally the people who can afford its hourly rate. This is not an accusation. It is an arithmetic. The median large-firm associate's hourly billing rate now exceeds what most American households earn in a week of work. The structural consequence is that the apparatus you spent three years and two hundred thousand dollars learning to operate is, in practice, available chiefly to corporations, the wealthy, and the institutional repeat players who can sign retainer agreements without flinching. Everyone else gets the public defender if they are criminal and lucky, the legal aid intake line if they are civil and patient, the law school clinic if they are within driving distance, and otherwise the unrepresented status that the trial court clerks call "pro se" with a small audible sigh.

Studies of the civil justice system — the Legal Services Corporation puts out one every few years — consistently find that roughly nine out of ten substantial civil legal problems faced by low-income Americans receive no legal help at all. Nine out of ten. The eviction notice arrives and no lawyer comes. The wage-theft claim sits in a desk drawer and no lawyer comes. The disability denial letter goes into the same drawer and no lawyer comes. The custody hearing is set for Thursday and no lawyer comes. The system you serve has, in its present configuration, decided that those clients are unaffordable.

You did not design this. You inherited it. But you operate inside it, and Isaias's imperatives — relieve, judge, defend — are addressed to the operator.

This is where Aquinas becomes uncomfortably useful. He is doing, in the article above, what scholastics do best: he is taking a question that the pious tradition had treated as a counsel and asking whether it is in fact a precept. May the well-off Christian give alms because he is generous, or must he give them because he is bound? Aquinas's answer, qualified and careful, is the second. When the giver has more than he needs for his own upkeep and the recipient is in a need that cannot easily be otherwise relieved, almsgiving moves from the counsel of perfection into the territory of obligation. Then he quotes Ambrose, who is even more uncomfortable: "Feed him that dies of hunger; if thou hast not fed him, thou hast slain him."

Translate this into the currency you actually trade in, which is hours. You have something the dying man needs. He needs a lawyer. The bar council assigned you a license that, in your jurisdiction, almost no one else can use on his behalf. You have a calendar. He has an eviction in fourteen days. The Thomistic question — was your time superfluous to your own upkeep, was his need such that no other relief was easily available — is not abstract. It is what your week looks like.

The honest answer for most working lawyers is layered. You are not a feudal lord with limitless leisure; you have your own family, your own mortgage, your own student loans, your own legitimate exhaustion. The hours are not all superfluous. But some are. Some hours of every week are. The hours you spend on the seventh draft of the M&A schedule that the partner will rewrite anyway. The hours of email that go nowhere. The Friday afternoon that drifts. Aquinas is not asking you to give until you bleed. He is asking you to look at the real surplus and not pretend it is not there.

The other half of his condition — "when the recipient is in such need that it is not easy to see how his need may be otherwise relieved" — is the part most lawyers cannot honestly evade. The recipient is in such need. Legal aid is rationed. The pro se clinic is overrun. The civil right to counsel does not exist for housing or family or benefits in your state, and the legislature is not going to create one this term. The need cannot easily be otherwise relieved. This is the condition, and it is met.

The point of opening the week this way is not to flatten you with guilt. Guilt is the wrong fuel. It burns hot and short and leaves an ashy taste, and after about a week of it the lawyer goes back to the calendar he had before. The point is sober vocational accounting. You are a member of a guild that monopolizes a certain practice. Most of the people who need that practice cannot pay for it. Christ in Isaias has told you what to do with the part of your time that is not strictly necessary for your own household. The doctors of the Church have told you, in increasingly direct sentences, that this is not optional.

So this week we will not start with heroic stories. We will start with the ledger. How many hours did you bill last week? How many did you give to a matter for which no one was billed? What is the ratio, honestly? Most lawyers, asked this honestly, will discover that the ratio is something like fifty to one, or worse. There is no judgment in the number itself. There is, perhaps, a question.

Isaias asked the same one in a different idiom. Learn to do well. He does not say be perfect. He says learn — which implies you do not yet know, and that the knowing comes from doing. The hand finds the work it has not yet done.`,
