    excerpt: {
      workId: "augustine-city",
      author: "Augustine of Hippo",
      citation: "City of God, Book XIX, ch. 21 (Dods trans., 1871)",
      text: "Justice is that virtue which gives every one his due. Where, then, is the justice of man, when he deserts the true God and yields himself to impure demons? Is this to give every one his due? Or is he who keeps back a piece of ground from the purchaser, and gives it to a man who has no right to it, unjust, while he who keeps back himself from the God who made him, and serves wicked spirits, is just? ... Where there is not this justice, there can be no assembly of men associated by a common acknowledgment of right, and therefore there can be no people, according to the definition of Scipio or Cicero.",
    },
    meditation: `The parable in Luke is told to people who are about to give up. Jesus prefaces it by saying so directly: we ought always to pray, and not to faint. The whole point of the story is endurance — the slow grinding kind that does not feel like prayer at all, that feels like nagging, that feels like the same complaint repeated until the listener gives in. The widow is the model.

Notice who she is. A widow, in the legal world of the Ancient Near East and Greco-Roman antiquity, was a woman without standing. She had no husband to bring her case, no son who could appear for her, no father whose name carried weight. Deuteronomy 24 — the chapter we will sit with tomorrow — names her specifically as one of the protected classes precisely because she had no protector. The widow is the figure for the unrepresented. She is everyone in any age who has no one to walk into the courtroom with her name on his mouth.

She does the only thing she can do. She goes to the judge. She does not have a writ. She does not have a lawyer. She does not have a brief. She has a sentence: avenge me of my adversary. She says it once. He ignores her. She comes back. He ignores her. She comes back again. And again. And the parable's strangest detail is that the judge — who fears neither God nor man, who is the worst kind of judge our literature has ever produced — eventually gives in, not because he became just, but because she wore him out. Lest continually coming she weary me. That is the line. The widow's victory is by attrition.

Jesus tells the parable to make a point about God, who is not the unjust judge but the opposite — the one who will quickly revenge his elect who cry to him day and night. But there is a second meaning curled inside the first, addressed to anyone who serves justice in the time before Christ returns. If even an unjust judge will eventually rule for the persistent widow, what will become of a judge who is just, but tired, and a system that means well, but is overloaded, when the widows in question can be outwaited? The parable assumes the widow will not be outwaited. In your courthouses, she often is.

Watch what happens in a busy housing court. The landlord's lawyer files the unlawful detainer. The tenant — a real widow, sometimes; more often a single mother with three jobs — does not appear on the first hearing date because her shift was changed at the last minute. Default judgment is entered. She comes the next week to explain. By then the writ has issued. By the time she finds the legal-aid office, the lockout is in two days. She has done a version of what the parable's widow did: she has come, she has come back, she has come again. The system, for her, runs faster than the parable does. The unjust judge of Luke 18 was at least there in the courtroom long enough to be wearied. The modern docket has built itself to be unwearyable. The widow falls into the gap between hearings.

This is the situation Augustine is reading the law into in City of God Book XIX, where he is at the most caustic point of his long argument with the late Roman political imagination. He has just finished quoting Cicero's old definition of a republic — a people associated by a common acknowledgment of right and a community of interests. Augustine asks where this acknowledged right could possibly live in an empire that does not give God his due. The classical definition of justice, he reminds us, is suum cuique reddere — to render to each what is his. If you cannot do that to God, who is the source of all that is, how will you do it to the widow, who is the most easily overlooked of all who are owed something? An order that cannot do justice to the visible widow because it cannot do justice to the invisible Creator is, in Augustine's blunt phrase, no people at all in the strict sense — only a multitude of men, held together by something other than right.

This is a hard reading. It is meant to be. Augustine writes it after Rome falls, when the rhetoric of imperial virtue had collapsed into ruin. He is asking what republic, if any, deserves the name. His answer is that a republic deserves the name only insofar as it gives each his due, and that the test case for whether a republic actually does so is the widow.

Bring this back to your week. The lawyer's role in the parable is interesting because the lawyer is not exactly any of the three named characters. He is not the judge — though he may someday sit. He is not the widow — though he stands closer to her than the judge does. He is not the adversary. He is, structurally, the figure missing from the parable: the one who could have been there with the widow and was not. The judge would have responded much faster if a competent advocate had been at her shoulder the first day, citing the statute, demanding the procedure, putting on the record the things that wear a judge down in twenty minutes rather than twenty weeks. The widow had to wear him down because no one was there to put the case on its proper footing.

So a great deal of pro bono practice is exactly this: standing where the parable assumed someone would not stand, and making the widow's case in the language the system actually responds to. You are converting attrition into argument. You are converting endurance into evidence. The widow's prayer — avenge me of my adversary — becomes, in your mouth, a Rule 12 motion, a request for a stay, a citation to the lease's habitability provisions. The substance is identical. The form is one the court can hear without being wearied.

Christ ends the parable with a question that should give every advocate a chill. When the Son of Man comes, shall he find, think you, faith on earth? The question is not first about doctrinal faith. It is about the kind of faith the widow had — the faith that kept her coming back to a court that was not listening. Christ's worry is that even his elect will give up, will stop coming, will conclude that justice is not actually available and that the docket is closed.

The lawyer who keeps showing up at the eviction docket, year after year, on the Wednesdays when nothing in particular has changed, is keeping that faith alive on behalf of the widows who would otherwise stop coming. He is the visible answer to the parable's last question. He is faith found on earth, in a courtroom, with a yellow pad. That is not too grand a description for what you do on those mornings. It is the parable's own description, in your robes.`,
